Here's the full audit of `SerializerTests.cs`.

---

## Test Anti-Pattern Audit — `SerializerTests.cs`

**Summary: 4 Critical · 0 High · 3 Medium · 0 Low**

This suite has a systemic problem: **5 of 8 tests contain self-referential or tautological assertions** that can never fail regardless of whether the production code is correct. Coverage numbers from this suite are meaningless — the tests touch code but don't verify behavior.

---

### 🔴 Critical — Tests giving false confidence

#### 1. `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 37–42) — Self-referential assertion

The input `"alice"` is *already* lowercase and trimmed. `Normalize()` does `.Trim().ToLowerInvariant()`, so it returns the input unchanged. The test asserts `input == result` — a tautology.

**This test would pass even if `Normalize` were a no-op (`return name;`).**

```csharp
// ❌ Current — tautological, input is already normalized
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// ✅ Fix — use an input that exercises the transformation
var name = "  ALICE  ";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice", result);  // hardcoded expected value
```

---

#### 2. `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 56–61) — Self-referential assertion (field vs. itself)

`GetDisplayName()` returns `Name`. The test asserts `user.Name == display`, which is `user.Name == user.Name`. The test name says "ReturnsFormattedName" but nothing is formatted.

**This test would pass even if `GetDisplayName()` returned any property of `UserDto`.**

```csharp
// ❌ Current — asserts dto.Name == dto.Name
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);

// ✅ Fix — assert against a hardcoded expected value
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);
```

---

#### 3. `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 72–77) — Self-referential assertion

The input `"HELLO"` is already uppercase. `.ToUpper()` returns it unchanged. The assertion `input == result` is a tautology.

**This test would pass if `ToUpper()` were a no-op.**

```csharp
// ❌ Current — input is already uppercased
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// ✅ Fix — use lowercase input, assert against a literal
var input = "hello";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

---

#### 4. `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 46–53) — Self-referential round-trip hiding data loss

The test asserts `entity == roundTripped(entity)`, but the mapping is **lossy**:
- `ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email`, hardcodes `Age = 0`
- `ToEntity` hardcodes `Id = 0` and `Email = ""`

So `backToEntity.Id` is `0` (not `1`) and `backToEntity.Email` is `""` (not `"a@b.com"`). **This test should actually fail** — if it's reported as passing, the suite may not have been run against the current production code.

Either way the round-trip pattern here is the wrong approach: a lossy mapping should be tested one direction at a time with hardcoded expected values.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — round-trip hides that data is silently lost
var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
var backToEntity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Id, backToEntity.Id);       // 1 vs 0 — should fail!
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Email, backToEntity.Email);  // "a@b.com" vs "" — should fail!

// ✅ Fix — test each direction independently with literals
[TestMethod]
public void ToDto_MapsFullNameToName()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age);   // document the intentional default
}

[TestMethod]
public void ToEntity_MapsNameToFullName()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, entity.Id);     // document the intentional default
    Assert.AreEqual("", entity.Email); // document the intentional default
}
```

---

### 🟡 Medium — Maintainability issues

| # | Test | Issue | Suggested fix |
|---|------|-------|---------------|
